;;; A rule's false `(not (eq …))` conclusion is stored as a negation —
;;; regression `not-eq-assert` (`corpus/regressions.toml`)
;;; (Q-M1e2.26).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(not (eq ?x ?x))` from `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(not (eq x y))` is `(neq x y)` (Q-M1f.31), and a rule's conclusion of one
;;; is checked (Q-M1e2.26): at root `r` concludes `(neq a a)`, which is false,
;;; so root is a contradiction, explained as `(false)` is: its core is `(p a)`
;;; and `refuted by` names `r`. R is empty.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `dc4c0bdc` (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`, release and debug
;;; alike), the conclusion was stored: root held `(not (eq a a) :rule r)` (`ein
;;; saturate FILE --dump`), no contradiction paired it, H was empty and R was
;;; {∅}, exit 0. The engine reads it as `(neq a a)`: R is empty.
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a node")
(p a)
(rule r ()
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (not (eq ?x ?x))
  :why "{?x} is not itself")
